  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 1153 AM MDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nMidday cumulus development is more expansive today, suggestive of\nthe modest increase in mid-level moisture regionwide. Near surface\nmoisture however is much more variable, with dewpoint fields \nexhibiting a considerable zonal gradient between the I-25 \ncorridor (~40-45F) and our eastern plains (~58-63F). Given\nlimited forcing, meager instability and drier low-levels, areas\nfrom the urban corridor westward will be limited to largely\nisolated high-based shower activity late this afternoon and\nevening, producing brief localized gusts of 25-35 mph. Over the\neastern plains, the warm and moist advection regime will hold\nsteady through tonight, leading to an increasingly buoyant\nenvironment with MLCAPE values of 1,500-2,500 J/Kg present across\nour northeasternmost counties by late afternoon. Thunderstorms\nlook to develop come this evening, with the bulk of the activity\nfavoring the late evening period coinciding with a moderate LLJ\nsetup over the plains. A few will be strong to perhaps marginally\nsevere, with an initial wind and hail threat that will \nnonetheless be tapered by the lack of shear, limiting overall \nstorm organization. Given the clustering potential and abundant \nmoisture (precipitable water values approach 1.5\" this evening), \nlocally heavy rain can be expected with any storms.\n\nWe'll gain a touch more moisture still on Sunday as moist\nsoutherly flow continues and the trough to our west inches\nslightly closer. The urban corridor also looks a little better\npositioned to tap into higher surface dewpoints, supporting\nmodestly unstable conditions for the afternoon. Thus, anticipate\nan increase in afternoon shower activity as well as potential for\nactual convection. There will be minimal change as far as\ntemperatures are concerned though, with most of the lower\nelevations breaking into the lower 90's once more.\n\nA slight waning in the high pressure on Monday will allow for the\nintrusion of stronger northwest flow at upper levels and thus some\nactual forcing, which should support a slight uptick in coverage\nof showers and thunderstorms, and likely the best precipitation\nchances of the next few days. Unsurprisingly, temperatures will\ncontinue to hold mostly steady.\n\nAt risk of sounding like a broken record, ensemble guidance still\nindicates a flattening of the ridge starting Tuesday (though more\npronounced Wednesday), followed by a westward shift of the ridge \naxis over Colorado. Slightly cooler temperatures - albeit still \nabove - should result from the initial flattening and transition\nto zonal flow, but this trend appears increasingly transient in\nnature, with ensemble guidance favoring a return to hotter weather\nfor the second half of the week. As previous discussions have\nalluded to, confidence in precipitation potential remains lower,\nalthough we still look to retain at least some moisture beneath\nthe ridge and thus a few opportunities for afternoon showers and\nthunderstorms.",
